          "q": "Fail-fast vs Fail-safe iterators in Java Collections?",
          "a": "Fail-fast iterators (e.g. ArrayList, HashMap, HashSet) check the collection's modCount. If modified concurrently while iterating, they immediately throw ConcurrentModificationException. Fail-safe (weakly consistent) iterators (e.g. CopyOnWriteArrayList, ConcurrentHashMap) operate on a clone or snapshot and never throw this exception.",
          "e": "To safely delete an element during iteration in fail-fast collections, always use iterator.remove() instead of list.remove().",
