      "code": "public class Test {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Integer a = 127;\n        Integer b = 127;\n        Integer c = 128;\n        Integer d = 128;\n        System.out.println((a == b) + \" \" + (c == d));\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        "true false",
        "true true",
        "false false",
        "Compilation Error"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explanation": "Java caches autoboxed Integer values between -128 and 127. 'a' and 'b' reference the exact same cached object in memory (a == b is true). 128 is outside the cache, so 'c' and 'd' are separate heap allocations (c == d is false)."
